00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:42.830
And he said, well, what about you?
他说，那你自己呢？

146
00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.270
And I said, oh, that's so great.
我说，哦，那太棒了。

147
00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:50.370
And there were 30 people up for the replacement for Dean Holland.
当时有30个人在竞争迪恩·霍兰德留下的空缺。

148
00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:54.850
Dean Holland went on to become the supervising producer and editor of Parks and Rec.
迪恩·霍兰德后来去担任了《公园与游憩》的监制和剪辑师。

149
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.290
And he left a hole that I was very happy to fill.
他留下的空缺，我非常乐意填补。

150
00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.170
I still remember my job interview.
我至今还记得我的面试。

151
00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:05.230
It was with Greg, Mike Schur, Paul Lieberstein, and Jen Salata.
当时在场的有格雷格、迈克·舒尔、保罗·利伯斯坦和简·萨拉塔。

152
00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:06.290
They were all in there.
他们都在那儿。

153
00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.910
And Greg was just, like, rapid-fire questions.
格雷格当时就像连珠炮一样问个不停。

154
00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:14.770
Luckily, I don't think I was nearly as intimidated as I should have been.
幸运的是，我觉得我当时并没有像我本该有的那样感到胆怯。

155
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:19.370
And I just didn't have anything to lose, because already I was never even considering this,
而且我没什么可失去的，因为我本来就没考虑过这份工作，

156
00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:20.930
because I'd never edited narrative.
因为我从来没剪辑过叙事类作品。

157
00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:22.430
I'd never, ever.
从来没有，一次都没有。

158
00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.810
I'd only done documentary and sketch comedy.
我只做过纪录片和小品喜剧。

159
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.810
And this was not in my wheelhouse.
这完全不在我的能力范围内。

160
00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.550
Which Greg probably loved, though.
不过格雷格可能很喜欢这一点。

161
00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:30.330
He loved that.
他很喜欢。

162
00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.690
He loved that I came with no baggage, he said.
他说他很喜欢我身上没有任何包袱。

163
00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:34.750
So I got the job.
所以我得到了这份工作。

164
00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.010
There was a little bit of, like, a let's see if it goes all right.
当时还有点那种“看看情况再说”的意思。

165
00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:38.790
And I guess it did.
我想结果还不错。

166
00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:44.130
Because at the end of season five, three of the three editors on The Office were nominated for Emmys that year.
因为在第五季结束时，《办公室》的三位剪辑师全都获得了那年的艾美奖提名。

167
00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:45.390
So everyone did well.
所以大家都表现得很好。

168
00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:52.470
And then Dave Rogers and I continued and edited all the way through the end of the—so I was there for about five years.
后来戴夫·罗杰斯和我继续合作，一直剪辑到剧集结束，所以我大概在那里待了五年。

169
00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.670
Started on season five and went through nine.
从第五季开始，一直做到第九季。

170
00:07:54.970 --> 00:08:02.030
Well, I just remember how seamlessly you fell into the fabric of The Office family.
嗯，我记得你融入《办公室》大家庭的过程是多么自然。

171
00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:06.790
Like, just your personality, your take on things.
就像你的个性，你对事物的看法一样。

172
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:08.410
It was an immediate fit.
简直是天作之合。

173
00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:18.090
Like, whatever you brought to that interview, it was clear probably to them and then immediately clear to the rest of us that you were like, oh, yeah, of course it's her.
不管你在面试时表现出了什么，对他们来说很清楚，对我们其他人来说也立刻很清楚，那就是：噢，没错，当然就是她了。

174
00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.010
It was wonderful to be there.
能在那里工作真是太棒了。

175
00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.930
I mean, it was a very atypical situation.
我的意思是，那是一个非常特殊的情况。

176
00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.090
I mean, I'm sure there's other situations like that.
当然，我相信还有其他类似的情况。

177
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:34.030
But to be all under one roof, to sit and eat lunch together, to have such pleasant working hours, cross-coverage that led to so many great comedic moments,
但能聚在同一个屋檐下，坐在一起吃午饭，拥有如此愉快的工作时间，还有那种促成无数精彩喜剧时刻的交叉报道，

178
00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:35.450
the electricity on the set.
片场那种充满活力的氛围。

179
00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.350
Like, I remember whenever something cool was happening, we would just be like, they're doing something cool.
我记得每当有什么酷事发生时，我们就会说，他们在拍酷东西了。

180
00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:39.750
Let's go.
走吧。

181
00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:40.050
Run.
快跑。

182
00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.490
And we'd run across the parking lot and go see something freaking cool.
然后我们就会穿过停车场，跑去看那些超酷的事情。

183
00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:45.330
Like, there's a porcupine in a drawer.
比如，抽屉里有只豪猪什么的。

184
00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:45.750
Run.
快跑。

185
00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.190
Rain's got a dream.
雷恩有个梦想。

186
00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.690
Like, so everyone just wanted to be part of the cool, fun thing.
总之，每个人都想参与到那些酷炫有趣的事情中来。

187
00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:51.270
Totally.
完全同意。

188
00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.370
I just want to watch it.
我只想看着它发生。

189
00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:53.930
That was the spirit of the show.
那就是这部剧的精神所在。

190
00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:54.650
It really was.
确实如此。

191
00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:55.510
It was like we were kids at school.
就像我们在学校里的孩子一样。

192
00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:55.890
Let's be a part of it.
让我们也参与其中吧。

193
00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.990
Someone was doing something cool in the art room.
有人在美术室里做着什么酷事。

194
00:08:58.290 --> 00:08:58.490
Yeah.
没错。

195
00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.710
You know, it was really special, and I've been chasing that ever since, you know.
你知道，那真的很特别，从那以后我一直在追寻那种感觉，你知道的。

196
00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:03.330
Same.
我也是。

197
00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:04.710
Yeah, same.
是啊，我也是。

198
00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.770
How did you come to direct this episode?
你是怎么开始执导这一集的？

199
00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:12.550
Had you said, you know what, I'd be really interested in directing an episode, or did someone come to you?
是你主动说“你知道吗，我对执导一集很感兴趣”，还是有人来找你的？

200
00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:17.450
I think that both Dean Holland and Dave Rogers led the way for editors directing on this show,
我想迪恩·霍兰德和戴夫·罗杰斯为这部剧的剪辑师转型导演开辟了道路，

201
00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:25.950
and I think it was a combination of those two gentlemen having paved the way and Greg's esteem for editors.
我认为这归功于那两位先生铺平了道路，以及格雷格对剪辑师的尊重。

202
00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:28.130
Like, he really is special.
他真的很特别。

203
00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:32.350
And I think that also has showrunners really do appreciate the editors.
我认为这也让剧集主管们真正地重视剪辑师。

204
00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:41.170
Like, a strong editor is someone you really are happy to have because it ultimately means less work for the showrunner and less hours in a dark room.
一位优秀的剪辑师是你梦寐以求的，因为这最终意味着剧集主管的工作量会减少，在暗房里待的时间也会缩短。

205
00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:49.830
So because they had done that, it was season eight, and I think there were some open slots, and I thought I'd just give it a go.
所以因为他们做了这些铺垫，当时正好是第八季，我觉得有一些空缺，就想尝试一下。

206
00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.950
And then I went to Paul and Greg.
然后我去找了保罗和格雷格。

207
00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:57.850
Okay, so now you mentioned that you edited your own episode.
好的，你刚才提到你剪辑了自己执导的那一集。

208
00:09:58.210 --> 00:09:59.810
I was a little surprised.
我当时有点惊讶。

209
00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.130
I wondered, like, if you would prefer it.
我在想，你是否更喜欢这样做。

210
00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.750
Do you prefer editing your directing?
你更喜欢剪辑自己执导的作品吗？

211
00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:11.990
So it's a very specific question for editing Angry Andy and whether I wanted to edit that versus today.
关于剪辑《愤怒的安迪》这一集，以及我当时是否想剪辑它，这确实是一个很具体的问题。

212
00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:12.550
Okay.
好的。

213
00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:17.550
So I absolutely wanted to edit Angry Andy in case I messed up.
我绝对想剪辑《愤怒的安迪》，以防我搞砸了。

214
00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:19.130
No one would know.
没人会知道。

215
00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:20.410
I could fix it.
我可以补救。

216
00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:21.850
You could cover it in editing.
你可以通过剪辑来掩盖。

217
00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:22.850
No one would know.
没人会知道。

218
00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.670
Like, how did she not get that other edit?
比如，她怎么会没拍到那个镜头？

219
00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.710
I'm going to just razzle-dazz you with something.
我打算用一些花招来糊弄过去。

220
00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:29.370
And you'll never know I didn't get that coverage.
你永远不会知道我没拍到那个镜头。

221
00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:33.310
I didn't get that shot that any more season director might have gotten.
我没拍到那个更有经验的导演可能会拍到的镜头。

222
00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:37.750
So I was very, very—I think I must have come in over the weekend so no one could even see me working on it.
所以我当时非常……我想我一定是周末过来加班的，这样就没人能看到我在剪辑它了。

223
00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:40.450
Today, I really don't think it's a good idea.
现在，我真的觉得这不是个好主意。

224
00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:44.250
I know Steven Soderbergh does it, so hats off to him and the Coen brothers.
我知道史蒂文·索德伯格会这么做，向他和科恩兄弟致敬。

225
00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.350
I think, especially in comedy, you can get a bit myopic and have set love.
我认为，特别是在喜剧中，你可能会变得目光短浅，对拍摄现场产生留恋。

226
00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.690
And I'm sure every actor feels this way, too.
我相信每个演员都有这种感觉。

227
00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:52.990
Something was hilarious.
当时觉得特别好笑。

228
00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:53.890
You know it hit.
你知道效果很好。

229
00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:54.910
The crew was laughing.
剧组人员都在笑。

230
00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:56.570
It took forever to set up the shot.
布置那个镜头花了好长时间。

231
00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:57.450
It all worked beautifully.
一切都进行得非常完美。

232
00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:04.110
And you just felt this kind of jubilation that isn't there when you watch it in the Avid on a television monitor.
你当时会感到一种喜悦，但当你坐在Avid剪辑系统的电视监视器前看回放时，那种感觉就不在了。

233
00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:04.810
It's just not there.
那种感觉完全消失了。

234
00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.890
And you have an editor be like, yeah, I don't care how long it took you to set up this shot.
然后剪辑师会告诉你，我不在乎你花多久布置这个镜头。

235
00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:08.370
It's not good.
它不好。

236
00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:09.090
It's not funny.
它不好笑。

237
00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:10.290
I didn't laugh, and I don't care.
我没笑，我不在乎。

238
00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:12.210
And you don't need to push the story forward.
而且你不需要强行推进剧情。

239
00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:18.090
And I like having that person that doesn't have, you know, attachment to it the way you might.
我喜欢有那样一个人，他不会像你那样对素材产生情感上的执着。

240
00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.290
The whole industry is a very collaborative one.
整个行业是一个非常讲究协作的行业。

241
00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:27.530
And I think that what was great about The Office is that everyone was firing on all cylinders and listening to everyone else.
我认为《办公室》最棒的地方在于每个人都全力以赴，并且愿意倾听他人的意见。

242
00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:33.770
I think that's the key is listening because you can have a lot of creative people, but if they don't listen to one another, it's going to be a train wreck.
我认为关键在于倾听，因为你可以拥有很多有创造力的人，但如果他们不听取彼此的意见，那将是一场灾难。

243
00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:34.550
Absolutely.
绝对是这样。

244
00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:36.550
And not only were they listening, they were encouraging.
而且他们不仅在倾听，还在互相鼓励。

245
00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.650
Like, everyone was a friendly ear.
每个人都是很好的倾听者。

246
00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:39.050
Yeah.
是的。

247
00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.330
And it wasn't scary in any way, shape, or form.
而且整个过程一点也不让人感到压抑或恐惧。

248
00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.030
And that's the set I walked onto.
这就是我加入的那个剧组。

249
00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:46.810
And, you know, Matt Sohn came in the weekend before to help me prep.
而且，马特·索恩在那个周末之前过来帮我做准备工作。

250
00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:49.730
That's the director of photography who also directed himself.
他就是那位也亲自执导过剧集的摄影指导。

251
00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:53.530
Jen Salata gave me this wonderful document.
珍·萨拉塔给了我这份很棒的文件。

252
00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:58.170
Okay, you shared this document with us, and our listeners know Jen Salata.
好的，你和我们分享了这份文件，我们的听众也认识珍·萨拉塔。

253
00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.210
She's been on the show.
她上过我们的节目。

254
00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:00.870
We love her dearly.
我们非常喜欢她。

255
00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:04.330
Will you share some of the advice that she gave you?
你能分享一下她给你的建议吗？

256
00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:05.450
I have to open up my computer.
我得打开我的电脑。

257
00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:06.790
You still have it.
你还留着它呢。

258
00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.370
You said you have it saved on your desktop.
你说你把它保存在桌面上了。

259
00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:09.730
It is.
是的。

260
00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.710
I mean, you can actually see it's on my desktop.
我是说，你其实能看到它就在我的桌面上。

261
00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.750
It literally says Jen's great advice.
上面写着“珍的绝佳建议”。

262
00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:15.970
And it kind of gives you an idea of the environment.
这让你对当时的环境有个概念。

263
00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:17.550
First-time directors don't get this.
初次执导的导演可没这种待遇。

264
00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:24.390
Like, first-time directors don't get, like, on a TV sitcom, don't get time with the DP over the weekend.
比如，初次执导的导演在拍摄电视情景喜剧时，周末是没法和摄影指导（DP）共事的。

265
00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:26.010
Like, DPs are exhausted.
摄影指导们都累坏了。

266
00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:31.070
This was the, I feel like, the creative collaboration that we always talk about.
我觉得这就是我们常说的那种创意协作。

267
00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:32.910
Everyone wanted each other to succeed.
每个人都希望对方成功。

268
00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:33.670
Totally.
完全同意。

269
00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:38.610
All right, so here are Jen Salata's advice, tips for you for directing.
好了，这就是珍·萨拉塔给你的导演建议和技巧。

270
00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:40.870
And, by the way, these are great for anybody.
顺便说一下，这些建议对任何人都适用。

271
00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:45.590
Number one, try to sleep beforehand, but absolutely do not worry if you can't.
第一，尽量提前休息，但如果睡不着也完全不用担心。

272
00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:47.810
Your adrenaline will kick in and take care of you.
你的肾上腺素会分泌出来，帮你撑过去的。

273
00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:48.430
I swear.
我发誓。

274
00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:50.130
I know.
我知道。

275
00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.410
I can see her saying, I swear.
我能想象她说“我发誓”的样子。

276
00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:51.710
Me too.
我也是。

277
00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:53.750
Two, be prepared as possible.
第二，尽可能做好准备。

278
00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.910
Figure out what you want to do, and then be okay throwing it all away.
想好你想做什么，然后也要做好随时推翻重来的准备。

279
00:12:57.290 --> 00:12:58.350
Both things really help me.
这两点对我帮助很大。

280
00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.370
Planning and the ability to adapt to better stuff that comes along,
既要有计划，也要有能力去适应随后出现的更好的方案，

281
00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:04.550
and just the vibe of being open to new stuff is good on stage.
而且在舞台上保持开放的心态去接受新事物，这种氛围很好。

282
00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:06.890
I still do that to this very day.
直到今天我依然这样做。

283
00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:08.490
That's great parenting advice.
那也是很好的育儿建议。

284
00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:09.050
Oh, it's two.
哦，这是第二条。

285
00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.250
A lot of these are going to adapt.
很多建议都是可以通用的。

286
00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:12.090
That's how I prepped for my weekend.
我周末就是这么准备的。

287
00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:15.850
I feel like a lot of these are going to apply to different facets of life.
我觉得很多建议都能应用到生活的不同方面。

288
00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.230
Three, directing, like, everything has a learning curve.
第三，导演工作和任何事情一样，都有一个学习曲线。

289
00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:21.750
Somehow people think that with directors,
不知为何，人们总觉得导演，

290
00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:24.210
they're just supposed to walk in on the first day and have all the answers.
第一天走进片场就应该知道所有问题的答案。

291
00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:25.390
Don't worry if you don't.
如果不知道，也不用担心。

292
00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:30.650
Four, for me, reading the chapter in Shot by Shot was helpful about the line.
第四，对我来说，阅读《Shot by Shot》里关于“轴线”的那一章很有帮助。

293
00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.230
She's talking about the don't cross the line.
她指的是不要越轴。

294
00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.110
And that's just kind of how you set up shots
这其实就是你如何设置镜头，

295
00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:38.430
and where you put the camera in relationship to the actor.
以及你如何摆放摄像机与演员之间的关系。

296
00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:40.270
She's like, but I can't imagine you need to do that,
她说，但我无法想象你需要做那个，

297
00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:41.570
but it was really helpful for me.
但它对我真的很有帮助。

298
00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.110
Editors have a pretty good idea about the line.
剪辑师对轴线很有概念。

299
00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:47.530
It looks really wonky if you cross the line in something that you're editing.
如果你在剪辑时越了轴，画面看起来会非常别扭。

300
00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:50.510
So I think inherently, you just kind of know where that line is.
所以我认为，你天生就能感觉到那条线在哪里。

301
00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:53.770
Yeah, I still don't think I understand the line.
是啊，我还是觉得我不明白那条线是什么意思。

302
00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:54.370
Don't cross the line.
别越界。

303
00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:57.330
It's like when you watch soccer and someone is off sides.
就像你看足球比赛时有人越位一样。

304
00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:58.310
I'll never get it.
我永远也搞不懂。

305
00:13:58.390 --> 00:13:59.190
I've let it go.
我已经不在意了。

306
00:13:59.470 --> 00:13:59.590
Same.
我也是。

307
00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:03.390
But it's okay because we have amazing people like you on set.
但没关系，因为片场有像你这样出色的人在。

308
00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:07.270
Don't cross the line doesn't mean don't do something inappropriate.
“别越界”指的不是别做不恰当的事。

309
00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:13.990
Cross the line is jargon in the industry about where your camera is placed.
“越界”是业内术语，指的是摄像机的位置。

310
00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:14.750
Where the camera is.
摄像机所在的位置。

311
00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:18.370
Number five, I drew shots on those set blueprints.
第五点，我在那些片场蓝图上画了镜头位置。

312
00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:22.170
I think she's referring to overheads, which is basically like the schematics.
我想她指的是俯视图，基本上就像设计图一样。

313
00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:25.790
Like when you're doing your house and somebody mocks up,
就像你装修房子时有人做模型，

314
00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:30.030
the architect draws a plan of your house and looks like a bird's eye view of your house.
建筑师画出你房子的平面图，看起来就像鸟瞰图一样。

315
00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:31.230
We have those.
我们有那些图纸。

316
00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:31.830
They're overheads.
那是俯视图。

317
00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:35.350
But Matt Soane recommended little dolls so I could place them and think about shots.
但马特·索恩建议用小玩偶，这样我就可以把它们摆上去，思考镜头怎么拍。

318
00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:40.730
So she took little dolls and she just kind of put them on the overhead shots.
所以她拿了一些小玩偶，把它们放在了俯视图上。

319
00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:42.730
Like at front reception or over at counting.
比如在前台或者会计部那边。

320
00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:42.910
Yes, exactly.
对，没错。

321
00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:43.910
So you could see it.
这样你就能看清楚了。

322
00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:47.150
So you could see where Angela would be and when she walked over to the jelly beans,
这样你就能看到安吉拉会在哪里，当她走到糖果罐那边时，

323
00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:47.790
you know, like.
你知道的，诸如此类。

324
00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:47.990
Yeah.
是的。

325
00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.290
Or better yet, where Ryan would be.
或者更好的是，瑞安会在哪里。

326
00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.050
Number six, I tried to get a wide shot of most scenes.
第六点，我尽量给大多数场景拍一个广角镜头。

327
00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.730
Some directors forget that, as you know.
如你所知，有些导演会忘了这一点。

328
00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:56.690
And it helped a few times.
这确实帮了几次忙。

329
00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:58.550
But then again, you know what you need.
但话说回来，你得知道自己需要什么。

330
00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:00.670
I didn't necessarily know what I needed.
我当时并不一定知道我需要什么。

331
00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:04.730
And I always, to this day, get wide shots or we also call them masters of the scene.
直到今天，我总是会拍广角镜头，我们也称之为场景全景镜头。

332
00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:06.310
Just you kind of know what the geography is.
这样你就能了解地理位置关系。

333
00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.550
Like, oh, Pam is at perception.
比如，哦，帕姆在前台。

334
00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:10.010
Angela's back there.
安吉拉在后面。

335
00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:12.150
You know, someone's walking out of the kitchen.
你知道，有人正从厨房走出来。

336
00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:13.950
You know, just kind of the geography.
你知道，就是那种地理位置感。

337
00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:17.910
So that when you go in tighter, you don't get surprised if you hear Oscar shouting
这样当你切到特写镜头时，如果听到奥斯卡在大喊，你就不会感到惊讶，

338
00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.710
and you didn't see him before.
因为你之前没看到他在那儿。

339
00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:21.130
I didn't know Oscar was there.
我不知道奥斯卡当时在那儿。

340
00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:23.410
As a viewer, you just kind of want to know who's in the room.
作为观众，你只是想知道房间里都有谁。

341
00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:25.230
Number seven, this is very true.
第七点，这一点非常正确。

342
00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:28.910
The actors like to do a few takes in a row, so try to get ahead of schedule.
演员们喜欢连续拍几条，所以要尽量赶在进度前面。

343
00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:33.210
And when I could, like not shooting a tiny nothing scene a million times could help me
只要我能做到，比如不把一个无关紧要的小场景拍上一百万遍，就能帮我

344
00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:37.890
make up time and have time for when the actors were pushing back and wanting to do more uninterrupted.
挤出时间，好在演员们要求重拍或想不间断地多演几遍时有时间应对。

345
00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:41.570
It was so special on The Office that we could do a couple in a row.
在《办公室》剧组，我们可以连续拍几条，这真的很特别。

346
00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:45.630
If you watch some of the bloopers, especially with John Krasinski and Rainn Wilson,
如果你看一些花絮，尤其是约翰·卡拉辛斯基和雷恩·威尔森的，

347
00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:47.710
they'll make each other laugh.
他们会把对方逗笑。

348
00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:53.370
And they would have this unspoken language and like acknowledgement where John would make
他们之间有一种心照不宣的默契，比如约翰把

349
00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:56.450
Rainn laugh, and then they would just start over again.
雷恩逗笑了，然后他们就会重新开始。

350
00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:01.030
And they would just keep resetting themselves and doing it over and over again until they
他们会不断地重置状态，一遍又一遍地重拍，直到他们

351
00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:04.690
got it right with no interference from the director at all.
完全没有导演的干预，做得恰到好处。

352
00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:06.750
And it's really interesting to watch.
而且看起来真的很有意思。

353
00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:09.750
And it was almost like they needed to get out their giggles.
感觉就像他们得先把笑劲儿过去一样。

354
00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:14.970
And then like on the seventh time they restarted, then they'd finally be able to finish the scene.
然后大概到第七次重拍的时候，他们终于能把那场戏演完了。

355
00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:15.930
That's like a dance.
那就像跳舞一样。

356
00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:17.890
I am really loving Jen's liSt.
我真的很喜欢简的清单。

357
00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:18.350
Same.
我也是。

358
00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:20.030
I can jump to eight.
我可以跳到第八点。

359
00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:23.250
Despite what anyone suggests, trust your instincts.
无论别人怎么建议，都要相信自己的直觉。

360
00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:26.110
You're smart and not afraid to ask for help advice when you need it.
你很聪明，而且在需要的时候不害怕寻求帮助和建议。

361
00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:28.110
This is Jen talking, not advice I got.
这是简在说话，不是我得到的建议。

362
00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.010
Smiley face.
笑脸。

363
00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:31.010
And then she wrote nine.
然后她写下了第九点。

364
00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:35.050
Oh, and some of the better episodes came when the directors made sure it all tracked, made
哦，还有一些比较好的剧集，是导演确保一切都衔接得上，确保

365
00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:35.450
sense.
逻辑通顺。

366
00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:37.730
She was amazing and said, you're going to be great.
她太棒了，她说，你会表现得很出色的。

367
00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:38.490
So excited for you.
真为你感到兴奋。

368
00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:39.210
We must celebrate.
我们必须庆祝一下。

369
00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:41.610
Again, very friendly first day of work.
再说一次，第一天上班非常友好。

370
00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:46.150
I do remember thinking to myself when we broke for lunch, okay, I can do this.
我确实记得午休时心想，好吧，我能行。

371
00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:46.890
I can do this week.
我能应付这一周。

372
00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:47.950
I've got this.
我没问题。

373
00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:50.590
And I remember the volume of questions.
我还记得当时问题的数量之多。

374
00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:52.950
And it's not like they're like all hard questions.
并不是说这些问题有多难。

375
00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:57.290
It's just the volume, the number, red cup or blue cup, blue pen, purple pen.
只是数量太多了，红杯子还是蓝杯子，蓝笔还是紫笔。

376
00:16:58.270 --> 00:16:59.870
This is like when you're the bride.
这就像当新娘的时候一样。

377
00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.710
I mean, it's lace or linen.
我是说，选蕾丝还是亚麻。

378
00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:02.610
I don't know.
我哪知道。

379
00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:03.630
Like napkins.
就像餐巾一样。

380
00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:04.670
You can do it.
你能做到的。

381
00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:08.130
And, you know, what I've learned subsequently is just always have an answer.
而且，你知道，我后来学到的一点就是，一定要给出一个答案。

382
00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:11.250
And then, you know, if you're like, I don't care if it's a red cup or a blue cup.
然后，你知道，如果你觉得，我不在乎是红杯子还是蓝杯子。

383
00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:12.450
Just have an answer.
但一定要给出一个答案。

384
00:17:12.610 --> 00:17:14.290
But to the proper person, that's their job.
因为对相关负责人来说，那是他们的工作。

385
00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:15.710
And it's really important for them.
这对他们来说真的很重要。

386
00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:17.710
And they've taken a lot of thought and care into it.
他们为此投入了很多思考和心血。

387
00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:18.990
To have an answer is good.
能给出一个答案是好事。

388
00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.070
I'll say red cup.
我会说红杯子。

389
00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:24.450
And then I'll go quietly, calm my mind, think about it and be like, no, the whole set is red.
然后我会悄悄走开，冷静一下，思考一下，然后觉得，不对，整个场景都是红色的。

390
00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:26.170
We need more blue in this, you know, shot.
我们需要在这个镜头里多加点蓝色，你知道的。

391
00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:30.110
I don't want it to, you know, seem like it's an angry scene because there's just the color red is too sharp.
我不想让它看起来像是一场愤怒的戏，因为红色太刺眼了。

392
00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.330
So then I go back and say, I've changed my mind.
所以我回去说，我改变主意了。

393
00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:33.110
I'd like blue.
我想要蓝色的。

394
00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:39.450
But the worst answer you can ever give is, I don't know, because that just makes you look like you're not good at your job.
但你最不该给出的回答就是“我不知道”，因为那只会让你看起来不胜任自己的工作。

395
00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:40.490
Oh, that's interesting.
哦，那很有意思。

396
00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:44.330
So make a choice and then reflect on your choice.
所以先做出选择，然后再反思你的选择。

397
00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:45.830
And if you need to change your mind, change your mind.
如果需要改变主意，那就改变主意。

398
00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:46.090
Yeah.
是的。

399
00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.810
It's better to be decisive and it helps people see you as a leader.
果断一点更好，这有助于人们把你视为领导者。

400
00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:50.310
Yeah.
是的。

401
00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.490
So at lunch, I was like, okay, I can do this.
午饭时，我心想，好吧，我能行。

402
00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:56.150
It's a lot of questions and it's a lot of pressure.
问题很多，压力也很大。

403
00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:57.130
I can do this.
我能行。

404
00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:58.790
Why would anyone want to do this?
为什么会有人想做这个呢？

405
00:17:58.890 --> 00:17:59.790
This is so intense.
这太紧张了。

406
00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:03.210
And then by the end of day one, I was like, I love this.
但到了第一天结束时，我心想，我爱死这个了。

407
00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:04.730
I only want to do this.
我只想做这个。

408
00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.470
Like, it was such a transition from halfway through the day.
就像是，从那天中间开始，心态发生了巨大的转变。

409
00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:08.310
I don't know.
我不知道。

410
00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.070
I think I just got used to it.
我想我只是习惯了。

411
00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.630
But I remember there was a halfway mark was like, I'm going to do this.
但我记得在中间的时候，我心想，我要坚持下去。

412
00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:14.430
I'm going to barrel through.
我要硬着头皮做完。

413
00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:15.230
I can do it.
我能做到。

414
00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:18.970
And then at the end, something shifted and I loved it.
然后到了最后，某种东西发生了转变，我爱上了它。

415
00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.730
I have a question for you.
我有个问题想问你。

416
00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.490
A big portion of this episode plays out in the conference room.
这一集有很大一部分是在会议室里展开的。

417
00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:30.930
And I noticed that you set up the table with chairs around it instead of doing the rows.
我注意到你把桌子摆好了，周围放了椅子，而不是排成排。

418
00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:34.850
And I went and I looked at the script for Angry Andy and it didn't say.
我特意去看了《愤怒的安迪》的剧本，上面没写。

419
00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:38.670
It just said everyone gathers in the conference room.
上面只写了大家聚集在会议室里。

420
00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:40.270
Did you get to pick?
是你自己选的吗？

421
00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:43.110
Did somebody ask you the question, table or rows?
有人问过你这个问题吗，是选桌子还是排成排？

422
00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:44.390
They do ask the question.
他们确实问过这个问题。

423
00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:50.290
I chose the table because I wanted it to feel more claustrophobic in the room, that people
我选了桌子，因为我想让房间里感觉更压抑，让人们

424
00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:55.030
were more stacked next to each other, that Aaron felt a little bit more trapped.
挤在一起，让亚伦感觉更被困住了。

425
00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:59.870
I also wanted it to make more sense that Andy doesn't see impotence on the whiteboard
我还想让安迪走进来时，没看清白板上的“阳痿”一词，

426
00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:01.950
and thinks it says importance when he walks in.
而误以为写的是“重要性”，这样显得更合理。

427
00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:05.010
It's there's not as much space because there's this big table in the way.
因为有这张大桌子挡着，空间没那么大了。

428
00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:07.690
So it's two people leading a conference.
所以这是两个人主持会议。

429
00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:13.610
It's more the people are all surrounded, this table, so that when people start talking about
更多的是大家都被围在这张桌子旁，所以当人们开始谈论

430
00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:17.870
crazy stuff like Phyllis's two-year-long orgasm.
像菲利斯那长达两年的性高潮这种疯狂的事情时。

431
00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:18.250
Yes.
没错。

432
00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:24.010
And then when finally Aaron's like, Toby, is there something that you can do HR?
然后当亚伦终于说，托比，你作为人事部能做点什么吗？

433
00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:25.230
Have anything to do?
你有什么事要做吗？

434
00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:26.850
And he's like, my job is nothing.
他说，我的工作就是无所事事。

435
00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:27.370
I have nothing.
我什么都没有。

436
00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:28.510
I can't do anything.
我什么都做不了。

437
00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:32.610
And you kind of feel like, you know, it's very stacked.
你会觉得，你知道，这真的很拥挤。

438
00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:37.930
And her ex-boyfriend is two people down from her when he tells her, you said having sex
而且当她前男友告诉她，你说做爱就像

439
00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:39.530
was like two skeletons wrestling.
两具骷髅在打架时，他就在她隔两个人的位置。

440
00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:40.030
Yeah.
是啊。

441
00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:43.370
So, which, by the way, was ad-libbed and made.
顺便说一下，那是即兴发挥的，而且做到了。

442
00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:44.490
I checked the script.
我查了剧本。

443
00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:45.190
I did, too.
我也查了。

444
00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:48.110
You felt like a skeleton was tackling you.
你感觉就像一具骷髅在扑向你。

445
00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:48.570
Yes.
是的。

446
00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:52.490
I checked the script because I noticed, Jenna, that you and I broke.
我查了剧本，因为我注意到，珍娜，你和我当时都笑场了。

447
00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:53.350
I noticed it, too.
我也注意到了。

448
00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:58.910
And I actually cover my face because Zach Woods is so good at improv.
我当时还捂住了脸，因为扎克·伍兹的即兴表演太棒了。

449
00:19:59.310 --> 00:19:59.630
Incredible.
不可思议。

450
00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:00.190
Incredible.
不可思议。

451
00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:00.970
So good.
太棒了。

452
00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:04.970
Which scene from this episode did you have to prep the most?
这一集中哪一场戏你需要做的准备工作最多？

453
00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:06.130
Can I guess?
我可以猜猜吗？

454
00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:06.950
Can we guess?
我们可以猜猜吗？

455
00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:07.730
What would you think?
你觉得会是哪一场？

456
00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:08.350
Go for it.
请讲。

457
00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:09.490
The horse?
那匹马吗？

458
00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:10.510
Because it had a horse.
因为里面有匹马。

459
00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:12.850
I was going to say the horse and all the...
我本来想说那匹马，还有所有那些……

460
00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:13.910
Or the punch the wall.
或者是捶墙的那场戏。

461
00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:14.290
Oh.
噢。

462
00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:20.250
I'd say the punch the wall because that required stunts, set deck, and props, as well as, you
我会说是捶墙那场，因为那需要特技、布景和道具，还有，你知道的，

463
00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:20.830
know, the glass.
玻璃。

464
00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:22.430
We had to make sure the glass wouldn't go...
我们得确保玻璃不会……

465
00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:23.730
Oh, on Nellie's picture?
噢，是内莉照片的那场吗？

466
00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:24.590
On Nellie's picture.
就是内莉照片那场。

467
00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:27.990
We had to be really careful about it because he really wanted to throw it down hard.
我们必须非常小心，因为他真的很想用力砸下去。

468
00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:33.390
So, it was a lot of the technicalities of that and putting your, you know, your hand,
所以，那涉及到很多技术细节，还有你的，你知道的，你的手，

469
00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.830
making sure that Ed wouldn't really actually hurt, and he'd done it before.
要确保艾德不会真的受伤，而且他之前也做过。

470
00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:37.890
So, that was good.
所以，那很好。

471
00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:39.470
But, like, he wouldn't really hurt his hand.
但是，他确实不会伤到自己的手。

472
00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:43.630
We did a couple of tests just, you know, with drywall squares just to make sure it was
我们做了几次测试，你知道的，就用干墙板，确保一切

473
00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:43.950
good.
没问题。

474
00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:49.010
I'd be so afraid in the moment I would miss the spot and I'd break my hand or something.
要是我在那一刻肯定会很害怕，怕砸偏了伤到手什么的。

475
00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:49.550
Yeah.
是啊。

476
00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:50.490
He was good.
他表现得很好。

477
00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:55.170
I think in relationship to where the nail was of Nellie's portrait, he knew exactly to
我觉得关于内莉画像钉子的位置，他很清楚该

478
00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:56.210
go just directly down.
直接砸下去。

479
00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:56.730
Jeez.
天哪。

480
00:20:56.890 --> 00:21:01.510
So, whereas the person that was the horse, whenever there's animals on set, I feel like
至于有马的那场戏，每当片场有动物时，我觉得

481
00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.390
the animals are there for a while.
动物们都会待上一阵子。

482
00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:06.870
That horse was really chill, a very slow, boring horse.
那匹马真的很冷静，是一匹非常慢、非常无聊的马。

483
00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:09.850
You know, like, it was really easy to manipulate that horse.
你知道，那匹马真的很容易控制。

484
00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:11.470
I feel like the handler was great.
我觉得驯马师很棒。

485
00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:15.950
One big element was that nobody that needed to act around the horse was scared of the
一个很重要的因素是，所有需要和马演对手戏的人都不怕

486
00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:16.290
horse.
那匹马。

487
00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:16.970
Yeah.
没错。

488
00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:23.610
So, you know, my dad works with horses, and he said that they can sense your heartbeat.
我爸爸是和马打交道的，他说马能感觉到你的心跳。

489
00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:26.490
And so, if you're nervous, it makes them nervous.
所以，如果你紧张，它们也会跟着紧张。

490
00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:27.750
So, that's really good.
所以，这真的很好。

491
00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:28.130
Right.
对。

492
00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:28.970
It was huge.
这很重要。

493
00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:30.030
It's a huge thing.
这是件大事。

494
00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.050
And horses are just, also, they have such empathy.
而且马，它们也很有同理心。

495
00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:32.510
Yes.
是的。

496
00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.950
That's why they do all of that great therapy work with horses.
这就是为什么它们会做那么多出色的马术治疗工作。

497
00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:36.330
That's what my dad does with horses.
我爸爸就是做马术治疗的。

498
00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:37.230
That's amazing.
太神奇了。

499
00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:39.750
The horses do therapy work with autistic children.
马会为自闭症儿童提供治疗。

500
00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:40.470
That's awesome.
太棒了。

501
00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:41.550
And he's one of the horse handlers.
他是驯马师之一。

502
00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:42.170
Yeah.
是啊。

503
00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:43.530
That's an amazing job.
那真是份了不起的工作。

504
00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:44.610
That's amazing.
太棒了。

505
00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:47.350
How gratifying, because I'm sure he sees the difference.
真令人欣慰，因为我肯定他能看出其中的差别。

506
00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:48.390
It's like night and day.
简直是天壤之别。

507
00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:48.610
Yeah.
没错。

508
00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.150
And horses are so smart.
而且马非常聪明。

509
00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:50.850
So smart.
非常聪明。

510
00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:51.470
So smart.
太聪明了。

511
00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:57.710
I noticed in the script that the throwing of the caramels was not scripted originally.
我注意到剧本里原本并没有扔焦糖这一出。

512
00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:01.810
Erin was just supposed to throw the pens and throw the messages.
艾琳本来只需要扔笔和扔纸条。

513
00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:04.670
And then she throws those caramels.
结果她却扔了那些焦糖。

514
00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:09.490
And I felt like, when I was watching, the reason I went to the script was because I felt
我当时看的时候，之所以去查剧本，是因为我觉得

515
00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:14.750
like Catherine Tate was legitimately surprised that she was having to dodge caramels.
凯瑟琳·塔特当时是真的被吓到了，因为她不得不躲避那些焦糖。

516
00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:16.390
Yeah.
是啊。

517
00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:19.310
I feel like everyone kept Catherine on her toes on that show.
我觉得在那部剧里，每个人都让凯瑟琳时刻保持警惕。

518
00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:20.610
Yeah.
没错。

519
00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:20.790
Yeah.
是啊。

520
00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:25.270
Well, was there anything else you prepped special for Angry Andy?
那么，为了执导《愤怒的安迪》，你还做了什么特别的准备吗？

521
00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:30.330
When preparing to direct Angry Andy, it was the first time that we would be seeing Nellie
在准备执导《愤怒的安迪》时，那是我们第一次看到奈莉

522
00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:32.090
as manager in that office space.
以经理的身份出现在那个办公室里。

523
00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:35.730
And that office space for the manager is so iconic.
那个经理办公室非常有标志性。

524
00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:36.550
Yes.
是的。

525
00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:38.530
And how it's decorated.
还有它的装饰方式。

526
00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:38.990
Exactly.
没错。

527
00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:39.990
With her so fun.
和她本人一样有趣。

528
00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:41.170
And her throw.
还有她的投掷动作。

529
00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:47.710
And I wanted to make sure that we had enough time to establish the space, but also keep things
我想确保我们有足够的时间来建立这个空间，同时也要让安迪的部分保持

530
00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:48.810
interesting with Andy.
趣味性。

531
00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:54.470
So a way in which to put the things in her office that set deck had deemed worthy of her.
所以要设法把布景部门认为适合她的东西放进她的办公室。

532
00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.290
And we also went through that as well.
我们也确实讨论过这一点。

533
00:22:56.290 --> 00:23:00.470
But also making sure they were placed in such a way that it could organically be revealed
但同时也要确保它们的摆放方式，能让它们在安迪和奈莉对峙时

534
00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:02.990
with Andy and Nellie having their standoff.
自然地呈现出来。

535
00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:03.350
Right.
对。

536
00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:04.510
Her saying, I'm not leaving.
她说，我不会走的。

537
00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:09.570
And then that's where she says, Angela, doc, $100 from Andy's back.
然后她就说了那句，安吉拉，医生，从安迪的背上拿100美元。

538
00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:16.590
Like crickets when Andy does the same, which is a wonderful payback for Angela to do to
当安迪做同样的事时，现场鸦雀无声，这对安吉拉来说是对安迪的一次绝佳回击。

539
00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:16.830
Andy.
安迪。

540
00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:21.770
And so definitely wanting to make sure that you really felt her presence and how thoroughly
所以我一定要确保你能真切地感受到她的存在，以及她是如何彻底

541
00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:24.470
ensconced she was into that new manager's office.
安顿在那个新经理办公室里的。

542
00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:26.210
And those scenes were tight.
那些场景非常紧凑。

543
00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:28.970
And you wanted it to feel like a high noon moment.
你想让它有一种正午决斗的感觉。

544
00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:30.650
Like they're against each other.
就像他们针锋相对一样。

545
00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:30.990
Yeah.
没错。

546
00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:35.450
And just making sure that in that small space, you still got that kind of awkward vibe of
只是要确保在那个狭小的空间里，你依然能感受到他们对峙时

547
00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:37.210
them facing off against each other.
那种尴尬的氛围。

548
00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:40.270
I thought the office was decorated so well.
我觉得办公室装饰得非常好。

549
00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:46.190
And it was very clear immediately that all of Andy's things had been wiped from the space
而且一眼就能看出安迪所有的东西都已经被清理干净了，

550
00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:48.530
and that it was a totally new space.
那完全是一个全新的空间。

551
00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:50.250
So I thought that was great.
所以我当时觉得那太棒了。

552
00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:50.770
Oh, cool.
噢，酷。

553
00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:51.550
I'm glad it worked.
很高兴它奏效了。

554
00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:51.950
Yeah.
是啊。

555
00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:52.370
It totally worked.
它完全奏效了。

556
00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:55.510
And actually, in re-watching it, I was like, oh, the office is different.
而且实际上，重看的时候，我就想，噢，办公室变了。

557
00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:56.750
And I was like, yeah, I see it.
我就想，是啊，我注意到了。

558
00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:57.870
You notice it right away.
你一眼就能看出来。

559
00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:58.190
Yeah.
是啊。

560
00:23:58.330 --> 00:23:58.410
Yeah.
没错。

561
00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:02.870
We always ask everyone, when the show ended, did you take anything?
我们总是问每个人，剧集结束时，你带走了什么东西吗？

562
00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:04.490
Because we took lots of stuff.
因为我们带走了很多东西。

563
00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:04.790
Okay.
好吧。

564
00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:05.690
Give it up, Claire.
快招了吧，克莱尔。

565
00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:08.270
I got permission from Greg to take.
我得到了格雷格的许可才拿的。

566
00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:12.470
And I feel now I need to pass it along because I feel like it's such a good thing.
而且我觉得现在我需要把它传下去，因为我觉得这真是一件好事。

567
00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:12.910
What?
什么？

568
00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:13.990
What did you get?
你拿到了什么？

569
00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.670
I got the ducky that would ruin takes.
我拿到了那只总是毁掉拍摄的小鸭子。

570
00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:18.150
Yes!
太棒了！

571
00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:19.090
I got the ducky.
我拿到了那只小鸭子。

572
00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:19.490
Oh, my God!
天哪！

573
00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:21.110
We haven't talked about this stuff.
我们还没聊过这些事呢。

574
00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:21.650
I have the ducky.
我有那只小鸭子。

575
00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:22.370
I still have it.
我还在留着它。

576
00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:23.150
It's on my bookcase.
它就在我的书架上。

577
00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:24.730
I think you do deserve that.
我觉得你确实配得上它。

578
00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.650
Either you or someone from sound deserves that duck.
要么是你，要么是录音组的某个人配得上那只鸭子。

579
00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:30.770
I felt like everyone's so many takes that I was like—
我觉得大家拍了那么多条，我就想——

580
00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:32.510
Made it hard for you to edit things.
让你剪辑起来很困难。

581
00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:37.550
But Sarah Levy was in the office when John burst in looking for something.
但莎拉·利维在办公室的时候，约翰冲进来找东西。

582
00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:38.870
He hadn't thought it through.
他当时没考虑周全。

583
00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:40.010
Like, this is over.
就像是，这一切都结束了。

584
00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:41.530
And he was like, what can I take when—
他就想，我能带走什么呢，当——

585
00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:43.050
And he took the sign off the wall.
于是他把墙上的牌子摘了下来。

586
00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:45.030
He started to go for the ducky.
他开始去拿那只小鸭子。

587
00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:47.350
And Sarah was like, Greg already promised that to Claire.
莎拉就说，格雷格已经答应把它给克莱尔了。

588
00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:51.250
So now I feel a little bit like,
所以现在我感觉有点，

589
00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:53.110
I've had my time with the ducky.
我已经和这只小鸭子共度过时光了。

590
00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.130
My kids have actually played with the ducky.
我的孩子们其实也玩过这只小鸭子。

591
00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:55.970
It still works.
它还能响。

592
00:24:56.290 --> 00:24:56.710
Oh, it does.
噢，真的吗。

593
00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:57.410
It still works.
它确实还能响。

594
00:24:57.590 --> 00:24:58.190
It's so loud.
声音太大了。

595
00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.150
Isabel would go hit it every time she was on set.
伊莎贝尔每次在片场时都会去按它。

596
00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:00.910
Because it's fun.
因为它很有趣。

597
00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:01.690
It's cute.
它很可爱。

598
00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:03.510
So many good bloopers with that ducky.
那只小鸭子贡献了太多精彩的NG镜头了。

599
00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.650
Well, one of the questions we always end an interview with is,
嗯，我们采访结束时总会问的一个问题是，

600
00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.790
was there any favorite memory from your time on the show?
你在剧组期间有什么最难忘的回忆吗？

601
00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:12.330
Anything you would want to share with us?
有什么想和我们分享的吗？

602
00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:14.870
I would say there's too many.
我想说实在太多了。

603
